# Portfolio content publishing

This rule applies to every content-based section of the portfolio, including Work, Projects, Writing, Field Notes, Sketches, Knowledge, Contributions, About, Credentials, and future content sections.

## Core principle

Publish broadly, commit selectively.

- A page may be deployed before it has passed final manual review.
- Git history is a durable record of content that has passed the author's review bar and is considered canonical.
- `published != committed`.
- `uncommitted != private`.
- Anything deployed publicly must be treated as public and potentially indexable, cached, archived, or copied.

## Content lifecycle

Real content generally moves through this lifecycle:

1. Existing source, note, or draft.
2. Restructure, sanitize, and adapt it for the public site.
3. Publish it to the live site.
4. Continue reviewing, editing, and improving it.
5. Commit it to canonical Git history only after it passes manual review.

AI-assisted rewriting, migration, restructuring, and cleanup do not count as approval.

## Git policy

- Do not automatically commit newly created, migrated, or AI-assisted real content.
- Before committing real content, confirm that it has been manually reviewed, meets the author's quality bar, and has had sensitive, private, confidential, or inappropriate material removed.
- Commit only versions the author is comfortable preserving in durable Git history.
- Do not bulk-commit generated or migrated content merely because it is already live.
- If approval is unclear, leave the content uncommitted and flag it for review.
- Do not automatically stage content changes. Do not commit or push unless the user explicitly requests that action.

This restriction applies to real content, not ordinary engineering changes. Layouts, components, styles, routes, schemas, loaders, configuration, search, Atlas integration, build/deployment tooling, tests, fixture/demo content, repository rules, and refactors may be committed normally when requested.

## Deployment policy

The site architecture must allow deployed content and canonical committed content to remain separate. Do not require a content file to enter Git history before it can appear on the deployed site.

Deployment is performed manually from the local working tree. A deployed artifact may therefore include uncommitted content; deployment does not imply manual approval or canonical Git status. The deployment process must not automatically stage or commit content.

When working on real content, preserve the separation between live deployment and canonical Git history. Content may remain live while it is being reviewed and remains uncommitted.

## Visitor-facing behavior

Editorial states are internal workflow concerns. Do not expose states such as `working`, `reviewed`, `approved`, `canonical`, `committed`, or `ready-for-Git` to visitors unless explicitly requested.

Visitors should not see different behavior solely because a page has or has not been committed.
